<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norse–Gaels</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gall-Ghàidheil, Gall-Gaedil, Norse-Gaels, Gallgaels, Gall-Gaidheil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Norse–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> were people of mixed Scandinavian and Gaelic descent and culture who emerged in Ireland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from the 9th century. They dominated the Kingdom of the Isles and the Isle of Man, and their Gaelic name, Gall-Ghàidheil, survives in Galloway. Rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godred-crovan">Godred Crovan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a> came from this world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norse-gaels.jpg" alt="Norse–Gaels" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Mann_and_the_Isles-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Gall-Goídil means foreigner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a>. Though it could in theory mean any Gael of foreign origin, it was used of Gaelic speakers with some kind of Norse identity. The Norse–Gaels arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> colonies of Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where from as early as the 9th century Norse settlers, except those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, married Gaels, took up the Gaelic language and many Gaelic customs, and left the worship of the Norse gods for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. They called themselves Ostmen or Austmen, the East-men, and called the Gaels Vestmenn, the West-men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Norse are first recorded in Ireland in 795, when they sacked Rathlin Island. After sporadic raids until 832 they built fortified settlements and set up kingdoms at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>, which did not survive the Norman invasions, though the towns prospered. Gaelicised Scandinavians dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> until the Norman era of the 12th century, founding the Kingdom of the Isles, the Kingdom of Dublin and the Lordship of Galloway, and briefly ruling the Kingdom of York from 939 to 944. Their most powerful dynasty was the Uí Ímair.</p><p>Among the English in Ireland between the 12th and 14th centuries the Ostmen were a group apart, given legal protections the Irish lacked for their place in the economy as merchants, fishermen, craftsmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raisers. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> they lived north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-liffey">River Liffey</a> in Ostmentown, now Oxmantown, and others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, until they merged with the English settlers. Norse–Gael settlers also took part in the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>, where Grímur Kamban, seen as the founder of the Norse Faroes, may have been one of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Most place names of the Isle of Man and the Outer Hebrides are of Norse–Gaelic origin, and the Hebrides are still called Innse Gall, the islands of foreigners, in Scottish Gaelic. The Lords of the Isles traced their descent to Norse–Gaelic settlements, as do Clan MacQueen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-donald">Clan MacDonald</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clan-macdougall">Clan MacDougall</a> and Clan MacLeod. The gallowglass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> came from these clans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> longship shaped the Gaelic birlinn, and surnames such as MacIvor and MacAskill survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norse–Gaels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Godred Crovan, a <b>Norse-Gael</b> who had fought at Stamford Bridge in 1066, seized the Isle of Man in 1079 after a battle at Sky Hill.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isles">Kingdom of the Isles</a></li><li><blockquote>Galloway takes its name from the <b>Gall-Gaidheil</b>, the 'foreign Gaels' of mixed Norse and Gaelic descent who settled the region in the 9th and 10th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-galloway">Lordship of Galloway</a></li></ul></section>
